// Technologies of 1925–2025. Contract and rules in crates/bf-sim/src/research.rs and
// docs/research.pt-BR.md. Names in assets/i18n/*/research.ftl (tech-<key>, tech-<key>-desc).
//
// Dates: `demonstrated` is the first time it worked anywhere, `industrialized` the first
// production at scale; both are the historical years, used to calibrate the 100-year run.
//
// Cost (effort to reach the science stage), by era: 0 ~20–60, 1 ~60–150, 2 ~150–450,
// 3 ~400–1050, 4 ~1000–2500, 5 ~2500–6000. Every technology after 1925 grows from at least one
// other, and costs at least 10 % more than each of its requirements: the tree reads left to right
// by level (two per era, pushed right by the requirements; see LEVELS_PER_ERA), each level asking
// for more research than the one before. R&D spending grew much faster than output, so the
// cost of a breakthrough grows about 2.5x per era; within an era, the big programmes (nuclear,
// computers, space) cost more.
//
// Productivity (per mille, per building group, full at the diffused stage). Target: the frontier
// gains about 1.5–2 % a year for a century. For each industrial group (extraction, heavy and light
// industry, energy, infrastructure, military industry) and for agriculture, the gains of the eras
// 1–5 technologies add up to sum ln(1 + g/1000) ~ 1.55, a factor of e^1.55 ~ 4.7 between 1925 and
// 2025 (1.55 % a year); services, ~0.5 (1.65x), because a barber cuts as much hair in 2025 as in
// 1925 (Baumol). How the numbers were set: each technology got a weight by how much it changed its
// sector (the Green Revolution, the robot and open-pit mining weigh the most), and the weights of
// each group were scaled together to reach that sum. Sums per group, eras 1–5:
//   agriculture: 1.544 (x4.68)
//   extraction: 1.550 (x4.71)
//   heavy_industry: 1.542 (x4.68)
//   light_industry: 1.545 (x4.69)
//   energy: 1.549 (x4.71)
//   infrastructure: 1.549 (x4.71)
//   military_industry: 1.547 (x4.70)
//   services: 0.498 (x1.65)
// Era-0 technologies carry small gains of their own (already in the 1925 output of the leaders,
// still ahead for the rest) and stay out of that sum. Methods change output too; this is the gain
// of the same method as the know-how around it improves.
//
// Effects (per mille, at the diffused stage): mortality and fertility are the share of deaths or
// births avoided; literacy, research and trade speed up literacy growth, raise research per
// researcher and cut the cost of trade. Provisional until the demography and trade phases.
//
// 1925 start: era-0 technologies have a default stage and the countries that differ; later ones
// are unknown, except those already understood (science) somewhere in 1925.
(
    // Ideas get harder to find: on top of the costs below, a technology costs this much more,
    // in basis points a year, for each year after 1925 its breakthrough came. The costs below
    // already grow about 4.75 % a year with the historical year (fit over the 143 technologies
    // after 1925); research productivity in the United States fell 5.1 % a year since the 1930s
    // (Bloom, Jones, Van Reenen and Webb 2020, AER 110(4), p. 1110), which leaves 35 basis
    // points. The value used, 350, is calibrated: the one that keeps the frontier dates of the
    // head-count research within a year. It is above the source because a laboratory buys a
    // researcher at the same price all century while his real cost rose
    // (docs/research.pt-BR.md, "Por que o calibrado passa da fonte").
    harder_per_year_bp: 35,
    technologies: [
        (
